OL,3,AMuseum agrees to return remains to
OL,4,Aancestral homeland
OL,5,
OL,6,FMore than three dozen sets of human
OL,7,Fremains taken during the British
OL,8,Fcolonial rule of India and Myanmar are
OL,9,Fset to be returned to their ancestral
OL,10,Fhome by a museum.
OL,12,FSkulls and bones belonging to the Naga
OL,13,Fpeople of northeastern India and
OL,14,FMyanmar - formerly known as Burma -
OL,15,Fhave been displayed at the Pitt Rivers
OL,16,FMuseum in Oxford for more than a
OL,17,Fcentury.
OL,23,APub: 11-SepQpppppppppppppppppppppG14/19
OL,24,ANext PageBCultureC Nat RadioFTechnology
FL,164,160,200,105,0,100
PN,11915
SC,0015
CT,25,T
PS,c000
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F119A1789201106
OL,1,W5j 5Q7+j 55"k#j 5w;js1
OL,2,W//%*// //%Q-.*,%-,* *,%%**,$ ,,,,,,,,,,
OL,3,AMuseum agrees to return remains to
OL,4,Aancestral homeland
OL,5,
OL,6,FIn 2020, the museum removed all human
OL,7,Fremains from public display as part of
OL,8,Fwhat the it called a "decolonisation
OL,9,Fprocess" - including the ancestral Naga
OL,10,Fremains.
OL,12,FThe museum has now announced that a
OL,13,Fformal decision has been made by the
OL,14,FUniversity of Oxford Council to return
OL,15,Fthe 39 ancestral human remains to their
OL,16,Fhomeland.
OL,23,APub: 11-SepQpppppppppppppppppppppG15/19
